          %p
            Moreover, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            is even a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            as above is a
            %em symmetric monoidal monad
            if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The notion of monoidal monad is equivalent to the notion of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strength
              for a monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is to give a monoidal monad whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
              monad is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToCommutativeStrongMonads Relation to commutative strong monads
          %p
            We discuss how monoidal monads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorially
            give rise to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monads"} strong monads
          %h4#strength Strength
          %p
            First to recall the notion of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon V \to V
            is
            %strong strong
            if there are given left and right
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+strength"} tensorial strengths
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            of the form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau_{A, B}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  A \otimes T(B)
                  \\to
                  T(A \otimes B)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma_{A, B}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  T(A) \otimes B
                  \\to
                  T(A \otimes B)
                  \\,,
          %p
            which are suitably compatible with one another: The full set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            conditions may be summarized by saying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            preserves the two-sided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+of+a+monoidal+category"} monoidal action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            on itself, in an appropriate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-categorical
            sense. More precisely: the two-sided action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            on itself is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax functor
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi V
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde{V}
                  \\colon
                  B V \times (B V)^{op}
                  \\to
                  Cat
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                denotes the 2-category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B V
                denotes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                one-object 2-category of the monoidal category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mi V
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B V)^{op}
                denotes its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+2-category"} 1-cell dual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphism"} 1-morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                (here:
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %li
              %p
                the two-sided strength means we have a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{V} \to \tilde{V}
          #SymmetricStrength.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In the setting where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
              monoidal, we will assume that the left and right strengths
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              are related by the symmetry in the obvious way, by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+square"} commutative square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi τ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi A
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mo
                                  %mi c
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mo
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi T
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi c
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
